(851581, 94865, 14656, '<td valign="top">Either my artistic vision is nowhere near everyone else, or I''m doing something wrong since I seem to only know how to displease DPC voters.<br/> <br/>The edge of the card was shown to give the appearance of a floating spacecraft or "planet." Perhaps I was unclear on that. I also added the border at the very last second (which was probably a poor decision) in attempts to draw more attention to the center of the image, thus cropping part of the bottom corner. My intent was to get the viewer to focus on the bokeh and lead the eyes towards the sharp foreground. I feel my score was hurt mainly by the edge of the card showing and the border.</td>', 'Either my artistic vision is nowhere near everyone else, or I''m doing something wrong since I seem to only know how to displease DPC voters. The edge of the card was shown to give the appearance of a floating spacecraft or "planet." Perhaps I was unclear on that. I also added the border at the very last second (which was probably a poor decision) in attempts to draw more attention to the center of the image, thus cropping part of the bottom corner. My intent was to get the viewer to focus on the bokeh and lead the eyes towards the sharp foreground. I feel my score was hurt mainly by the edge of the card showing and the border.', '2004-08-09 20:52:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(851634, 94677, 11793, '<td valign="top">well done.<br/></td>', 'well done.', '2004-08-09 21:47:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(851733, 94235, 22734, '<td valign="top">From the critique club:<br/> <br/>Very nice composition because it starts center bottom, sweeps the left then coils to right and heads back and away to end at top right. This is a universal pattern associated with the serpent, the  coil and certain bacteria.<br/>  The strenght of this picture is the unindentified article. My wild guess is that its texture appears to be metal. If it is metal I would guess it is the high tech barbed wire...yet I see a varying width which brings me back to first base. So the interesting shape and unknown item make this a very interesting study. It is a nice composition.<br/> <br/>  The shallow dof is the only major draw back. If we can easily identify the object, then the dof is not critical. If we can''t, then the eyes want to examine every inch of the item and the viewers battle is quickly lost the moment they begin to study the image. It is more like a let down. Wow, what a shape? What is it? Let me see...uhmm focus is already gone past the middle coil on the right.<br/> <br/>  While the image is nice as is, this is an ideal subject which may be enhanced with a minor backlight, this would have added more mystery.<br/> <br/>To conlude your idea was very nice, composition above average, but the execution of the quality, mainly the dof is the culprit. Always envision the shot in your minds eye and then do what ever is technically required. Normal shots and distant scenes differ greatly from the close up. A camera in auto mode, will never make this decision for you, so it is up to you to take care of this matter. The easiest way to do it is to make a test shot, check it in computer and then go back to finish. <br/>                                                             dan</td>', 'From the critique club: Very nice composition because it starts center bottom, sweeps the left then coils to right and heads back and away to end at top right. This is a universal pattern associated with the serpent, the  coil and certain bacteria.  The strenght of this picture is the unindentified article. My wild guess is that its texture appears to be metal. If it is metal I would guess it is the high tech barbed wire...yet I see a varying width which brings me back to first base. So the interesting shape and unknown item make this a very interesting study. It is a nice composition.   The shallow dof is the only major draw back. If we can easily identify the object, then the dof is not critical. If we can''t, then the eyes want to examine every inch of the item and the viewers battle is quickly lost the moment they begin to study the image. It is more like a let down. Wow, what a shape? What is it? Let me see...uhmm focus is already gone past the middle coil on the right.   While the image is nice as is, this is an ideal subject which may be enhanced with a minor backlight, this would have added more mystery. To conlude your idea was very nice, composition above average, but the execution of the quality, mainly the dof is the culprit. Always envision the shot in your minds eye and then do what ever is technically required. Normal shots and distant scenes differ greatly from the close up. A camera in auto mode, will never make this decision for you, so it is up to you to take care of this matter. The easiest way to do it is to make a test shot, check it in computer and then go back to finish.                                                              dan', '2004-08-09 22:49:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(851779, 94228, 14819, '<td valign="top">heh, how many bottles did it take?</td>', 'heh, how many bottles did it take?', '2004-08-09 23:20:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(851787, 95183, 2333, '<td valign="top">Thanks to all, this was the one and only shot from about 1.5 hours of shooting out in the back yard that was even remotely challenge-worthy. Still, I really need to work on my lighting, and maybe catch some dragonflies if I''m ever going to best Jacko, LOL!<br/> <br/>I also wanted to add some additional details that I forgot to include during submission...<br/> <br/>This guy was busy having dinner on some weeds in a bck corner of our land. I still had good illumination from late afternoon sun, but with 1:1 magnification, I still needed quite a bit of fill flash. The 1/60 shutter speed preserved some of the ambient light, at the cost of a very slight blur on the thorax, which is only slightly visible in this reduced-size version. He would not make a very good large print however.<br/> <br/>With the combination of title and image, I was going the "anthromorphic route", that is assigning human emotions/reactions to the subject. I imagined his thoughts on the fact that I disturbed the priviacy of his dinner with my photographic intrusion... can you look in his eyes and imagine his thoughts? Yikes!<br/><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-08-09 22:38:23.</i></td>', 'Thanks to all, this was the one and only shot from about 1.5 hours of shooting out in the back yard that was even remotely challenge-worthy. Still, I really need to work on my lighting, and maybe catch some dragonflies if I''m ever going to best Jacko, LOL! I also wanted to add some additional details that I forgot to include during submission... This guy was busy having dinner on some weeds in a bck corner of our land. I still had good illumination from late afternoon sun, but with 1:1 magnification, I still needed quite a bit of fill flash. The 1/60 shutter speed preserved some of the ambient light, at the cost of a very slight blur on the thorax, which is only slightly visible in this reduced-size version. He would not make a very good large print however. With the combination of title and image, I was going the "anthromorphic route", that is assigning human emotions/reactions to the subject. I imagined his thoughts on the fact that I disturbed the priviacy of his dinner with my photographic intrusion... can you look in his eyes and imagine his thoughts? Yikes!', '2004-08-09 23:24:54.000000', '2004-08-09 23:38:23.000000', 0),
