(852479, 95314, 16230, '<td valign="top">In case any was wondering...<br/>Dawn dish soap, red food coloring, and water mixed in a black plastic cereal bowl. Air blown through a straw created the bubbles. Two AlienBees B400 strobes in medium Octaboxes, one on either side, provided the light.<br/> <br/></td>', 'In case any was wondering...Dawn dish soap, red food coloring, and water mixed in a black plastic cereal bowl. Air blown through a straw created the bubbles. Two AlienBees B400 strobes in medium Octaboxes, one on either side, provided the light. ', '2004-08-10 12:52:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(852480, 93797, 13933, '<td valign="top">Beautiful. I love macros, esp bugs!</td>', 'Beautiful. I love macros, esp bugs!', '2004-08-10 12:52:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(852497, 94228, 14476, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by graphicfunk:</b></div><hr/><i> The initial test shot was made with water. Placed cork on wire and duct taped wire to pail for multiple takes. Filled bottle and poured over cork and of course flipped picture vertically. <br/> Went back with colored water and dish detergent. After 30 takes, I gave up and examined the takes. The original test shot was uploaded with the water because it had the best splash and it covered the wire completely. Action captured with two side strobes bouned off white reflectors and a frontal fresnel as frontal fill.<br/> <br/>I felt guilty because of the water and changed the name from "Pop" to "Pop, a macro ilussion", hoping that the viewer would forgive me for using plain water. I figured illusion implies an effect rather than a reality. Anyway, thank you all for your kind response, dan </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Oh!  That''s how you did it!  I dunno what a "frontal fresnel" is (having heard tha term only in association with lighthouses) but it certainly is a splendid effect.</td>', 'Originally posted by graphicfunk: The initial test shot was made with water. Placed cork on wire and duct taped wire to pail for multiple takes. Filled bottle and poured over cork and of course flipped picture vertically.  Went back with colored water and dish detergent. After 30 takes, I gave up and examined the takes. The original test shot was uploaded with the water because it had the best splash and it covered the wire completely. Action captured with two side strobes bouned off white reflectors and a frontal fresnel as frontal fill. I felt guilty because of the water and changed the name from "Pop" to "Pop, a macro ilussion", hoping that the viewer would forgive me for using plain water. I figured illusion implies an effect rather than a reality. Anyway, thank you all for your kind response, dan   Oh!  That''s how you did it!  I dunno what a "frontal fresnel" is (having heard tha term only in association with lighthouses) but it certainly is a splendid effect.', '2004-08-10 13:10:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(852738, 94485, 18419, '<td valign="top">Thanks everyone. I think Imagineer sumed it up best (and very politely!)  This was one of my first go''s at a ''studio'' type shot, and couldn''t get the lighting quite right.  Plus, the choice of a white background probably wasn''t the best choice.<br/> <br/>I''m off to the ''Reasons why my shot didn''t turn out'' thread for more excuses!<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/>Stu<br/></td>', 'Thanks everyone. I think Imagineer sumed it up best (and very politely!)  This was one of my first go''s at a ''studio'' type shot, and couldn''t get the lighting quite right.  Plus, the choice of a white background probably wasn''t the best choice. I''m off to the ''Reasons why my shot didn''t turn out'' thread for more excuses! CheersStu', '2004-08-10 16:15:37.000000', NULL, 0),
